Root cause:
The bug was caused by the incorrect handling of mouse transparency during the transition animation. Specifically, the previous implementation attempted to manage the mouse transparency of the `previousNode` instead of the entire `view`, which could lead to unintended user interactions with the `previousNode` while it was still visually present during the transition. This oversight likely resulted in a confusing user experience, as the `previousNode` could still receive mouse events even when it was not intended to be interactive.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
previousNode.setMouseTransparent((Boolean) previousNode.getProperties().get(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT));
previousNode.setMouseTransparent(true);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code incorrectly manages the mouse transparency of the `previousNode` instead of the entire `view`. This allows the `previousNode` to still receive mouse events during the transition, leading to unintended user interactions.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
view.setMouseTransparent(false);
view.setMouseTransparent(true);
```
Reason:
The fix changes the mouse transparency handling to apply to the entire `view` instead of just the `previousNode`. By setting `view.setMouseTransparent(true)`, it ensures that the whole view is non-interactive during the transition, preventing any unintended interactions with the `previousNode`.


